ns.WhatsNewData = {
	{
		version = "0.5.45",
		lines = {
			"\001/flatui move\002: press on empty space and drag to select every box the rectangle touches, but for the boxes of frames the game places. It has a thin accent outline over a faint fill, and the boxes light up as you drag. Shift and drag adds them to the selection, Ctrl and drag takes them away, and a click on empty space lets go of them all. Shift or Ctrl and click on a box, or on a name in the frame list, adds it or takes it away; a plain click still selects one box alone.",
			"\001/flatui move\002: every selected box has the accent outline and every selected frame is marked in the frame list. Drag any of them and the others move with it by the same amount, keeping their places to one another and stopping together at the edge of the screen. With \001Snap\002 on, the box under the mouse snaps and the others follow it. The arrow keys move them all, Shift ten at a time.",
			"\001/flatui move\002: with two or more selected, the inspector reads \"3 frames selected\" and offers \001Align left\002, \001Align right\002, \001Align top\002, \001Align bottom\002, \001Centre horizontally\002 and \001Centre vertically\002 (on the middle of the space they cover together). It also has \001Distribute across\002 and \001Distribute down\002 (equal gaps in their order on the screen, with three or more) and \001Reset these\002.",
			"\001/flatui move\002: each group drag, align, distribute or \001Reset these\002 is one Undo step, and so is a run of arrow presses less than a second apart. Undo and Redo select the frames they put back. Hidden frames, frames not on screen now and frames placed by the game stay put in group moves and in \001Reset these\002; the inspector says how many, and chat says once which.",
			"\001/flatui move\002: Escape now lets go of the selection first and closes the boxes once nothing is selected. Ctrl and click no longer marks boxes for the lower \001Space across\002 and \001Space down\002 bar, which is gone: \001Distribute across\002 and \001Distribute down\002 in the inspector do the same for the selected frames.",
		},
	},
	{
		version = "0.5.44",
		lines = {
			"Nameplates: Enlarge enemies you are losing (on by default, under Mark enemies you are losing on the Threat tab). While you tank, the nameplate of an enemy on someone else grows to Size of an enemy you have lost (130% by default, from 100% to 160%), and one changing hands grows half as much. It eases over a fifth of a second about its own centre so it does not jump, rises above the plates around it, and shrinks back the same way as you take the enemy back.",
			"Nameplates: where the game keeps a plate from being changed in a fight, the plate keeps its size and the glow and arrow of Mark enemies you are losing grow by the same amount instead; the plate grows once the fight ends if the enemy is still lost.",
			"Nameplates: a plate the game hands to another enemy, a plate that hides, either row turned off, no longer counting as a tank and Flat UI's nameplates turned off all put the plate's size and layer back as they were.",
			"Nameplates: test mode's sample nameplate and the samples of Setup's Nameplate colours and threat step grow as live plates do on threat you do not want, and half as much on threat changing hands; Setup makes room for them and keeps each label beside its sample.",
			"Threat list: your group on your target is back for everyone, as before 0.5.43. \001List\002 has a new default, \001Your target, then enemies you are losing\002. It shows your group on your target with all its rows, colours, warnings and sounds. While you tank it also adds, under a thin line, up to 4 small rows for the other enemies you are losing: Lost ones first, then Slipping ones. Each row has the enemy's raid marker, its name, its colour and pulse, and who holds it or is closing in.",
			"Threat list: while one of those enemies is lost, the title adds a small \"· 1 lost\" after your target's name, as in \"Defias Pillager · 1 lost\". Nothing is added while no other enemy is lost or slipping, so a fight against one enemy looks as it always did.",
			"Threat list: the small rows count apart from \001Number of bars\002. A row flashes and plays \001Sound when you lose an enemy\002 when its enemy turns to Lost, and the rows move with \001Animate bars\002. Your target itself still has \001Warn when you lose aggro\002.",
			"Threat list: \001Enemies while you tank\002, the 0.5.43 default, is now \001Your target, then enemies you are losing\002, and profiles that had it move to the new choice. \001Enemies you fight\002 still shows only the enemies, and \001Your group on your target\002 shows your group on your target alone, without the enemies under it.",
			"Threat list: the enemies under your target are read at most five times a second, only in a fight and only while you tank. Test mode and /flatui threat show two sample enemies under the sample group, one lost and one slipping. In test mode the slipping one turns lost every 6 seconds.",
			"The tips of \001Calm when another tank takes it\002 and \001Show healer mana\002 now say \"they\" and \"their\" for the other player.",
			"\001Search\002 heads each group of results, and each page chip, with the page's place, as \"Flat UI > Colours\" and \"Nameplates > Colours\" in place of two groups both called \"Colours\", and a module's name now counts as a word of each of its tabs, so \"/flatui nameplate cast colour\" finds the colour rows of \001Nameplates > Cast bar\002; a tab's chip shows only when its module's chip does not already match.",
			"Typing \001#changed\002 in \001Search\002, or /flatui #changed, lists every setting of the profile in use that is off its default, grouped by page, each with its reset arrow; the head says how many (\"37 changed settings\"), or \"Every setting is at its default.\" A dim \001Every change\002 in Home's \001Recent changes\002 opens it, and pointing at the Search box says the word exists.",
			"With \001Replace the game's fonts\002 on and the options window open, changing a row that is not a Fonts row in a fight (\001Border colour\002, \001Bar texture\002...) no longer says \"Flat UI: fonts change when the fight ends.\"; the line shows only when a \001Fonts\002 row really changed.",
			"\001Export by module\002 has \001All\002 and \001None\002 above its module list, so None and one tick export a single module; the \001Modules\002 button reads \001Hide list\002 while the list is open, and \001Export chosen\002 with nothing ticked says \"Tick a module first.\"",
			"A right-click on any colour swatch offers \001Copy #D93A30\002 and, once a colour is copied, \001Paste #D93A30\002, which writes it at once, shows in \001Recent changes\002 and is undone with Ctrl+Z; the copied colour also leads the colour picker's recent colours.",
			"The test mode bar now opens with the \001Group\002 last picked on the account and at the place you dragged it to, also after a reload; a double-click on its title still puts it back and forgets the drag.",
			"A double-click on the options window's title bar puts the window back in the middle of the screen and forgets its saved place (\"The window is back in the middle.\").",
			"The test mode bar fades in and out instead of appearing and vanishing at once, and turning test mode on again while it fades out brings it straight back.",
			"/flatui profile answers now start with \"Flat UI:\", and a name it does not know lists the profiles: \"Flat UI: no profile named Raidd. Profiles: Default, Raid, Alt.\"",
			"\001Bar block\002: a block whose bars were moved or added now shows the changed dot and reset arrow on its \001Bars\002 row (\"Reset Bars: back to its default.\"), and the \"changed on this tab\" count goes to it, where before the count and the dots led to no row.",
			"\001Show\002, under \001Health and power text\002, has a third choice, \001While not full\002. The health text appears as soon as a unit takes damage and goes again once they are topped up, and the power text does the same for mana, also in a fight, so a party column stays clean until someone needs a heal. Dead, Ghost and Offline still always show. Setup's sample follows it.",
			"\001Colour health bars by health left\002 now also colours raid cells and raid-style party frames from green through yellow to red, also in a fight. Turned off, they take the game's colours back, and offline members keep the game's grey. Setup's \001Boss and raid frames\002 step draws the cells in those colours. The tip no longer says \"Not raid frames.\"",
			"The AFK count now stays with the member. Before, a raid cell that moved when someone joined or left started again at a bare \"AFK\", and on party frames the member who moved into party1 could take over the old member's \"AFK 12m\". Party and raid frames now agree. In a raid fight, frames no longer listen to every health change while nobody is away.",
			"\001AFK on raid frames\002: while the game's raid frame \001Display Health Text\002 shows health (Health, Health Lost or Percent), the AFK word now sits just above it in the middle of the cell instead of on top of it. With None it stays where the status text sits.",
			"Nameplate \001Low health border\002: a nameplate that first appears in a fight now queues one catch-up job, not one per health change, so the end of a long fight no longer hitches. With the options window open, chat no longer says \"the low health border shows when the fight ends.\" for nameplates, where no setting changed.",
			"\001Five-second rule\002: as the spark reaches the end of the mana bar it now flares, growing to about 1.6 times its size as it fades over 0.3 seconds, so you see regen come back. A spell cast before in the session still starts the sweep in a fight where the game withholds its cost. Test mode shows the flare in its one second rest.",
			"Changing \001Frame style\002, \001Portrait size\002, \001Space between parts\002, \001Name room\002, \001Level position\002, \001Corners\002 or \001Portrait shape\002 in a fight now says once, while the options window shows, \"Flat UI: unit frames take their new layout when the fight ends.\" \001Shadow behind each frame\002 says \"Flat UI: frame shadows show when the fight ends.\" and \001Custom pet frame size\002 says \"Flat UI: the pet frame takes its new size when the fight ends.\" A new target without a power bar or a pet summoned in a fight stays silent. The heal over time squares' \"show\" and \"move\" lines are now each said on their own, so one no longer hides the other in a fight.",
			"\001Highlight under the mouse\002 now eases in over 0.08 seconds and out over 0.15 as the mouse sweeps down the party or raid frames, instead of snapping on and off. With \001Show\002 at \001Under the mouse\002, the health and power text now fades in over 0.1 seconds and out over 0.15.",
			"Raid markers on party and raid frames now pop from about 1.6 times their size to their own over 0.2 seconds as one lands or changes, also in a fight, so a Skull or Moon placed on a member is noticed. Nothing pops at login, on zoning, on joining a group or for a new member in a cell.",
			"\001Squares for your heals over time\002: a square that lights as your heal lands, or is refreshed with a later end, now pops from about 1.5 times its size over 0.15 seconds, so a healer sees which frame took the heal. Squares read for the first time, at login, for a new member or as a fight ends, do not pop.",
			"New \001Enemy nameplates\002, \001Friendly player nameplates\002 and \001Friendly NPC nameplates\002 (off, as the game has them at first) and \001Always show nameplates\002 (on), at the top of \001Which show\002 on the \001Size and clicks\002 tab of \001Nameplates\002. These are the game's own switches, so plates that vanished after pressing V, or friendly plates for a battleground, can come back without leaving Flat UI. Each shows the game's live value, also after V or Shift+V. \001Nameplates on enemy pets and totems\002 and \001Nameplates on minor enemies\002 now grey out while Enemy nameplates is off, and \001Friendly players as names only\002 while Friendly player nameplates is off, as the game nests them.",
			"New \001Stack enemy nameplates\002 and \001Stack friendly nameplates\002 (off), under \001Size and stacking\002. They turn on the game's stacking, off on this client at first. The plates of a pulled pack now move apart up and down into a readable column instead of covering each other, and \001Horizontal overlap\002 and \001Vertical overlap\002 set their spacing while they stack. Setup's \001Nameplate size, distance and clicks\002 drawing shows the near plate over the target while Stack enemy nameplates is off and stacked above it while it is on.",
			"New \001Class colours on enemy players\002 and \001Class colours on friendly players\002 (off), under \001Reaction\002 on the \001Colours\002 tab of \001Nameplates\002. They turn on the game's class colours for players' health bars, a rogue yellow and a mage light blue, and nameplates repaint at once. The \001Players\002 tip now points to them. Setup's \001Enemy player\002 samples are hostile red while the enemy row is off. Before, they always took your own class colour. They take a class colour while it is on, the \001Friendly\002 samples follow the friendly row, and the colours drawing adds a \001Friendly player\002 sample while that row is on.",
			"New \001Aura size\002 (100%, 70% to 140%), on the \001Auras\002 tab of \001Nameplates\002. It sets the game's size of the buff and debuff icons on nameplates, so larger Rend, Sap or Rejuvenation icons need no trip to the game's options. The Overpower icon, an \001Above\002 threat value and an \001Above\002 quest mark rise with the taller row, and setup's \001Nameplate auras\002 drawing and preview band grow with it.",
			"New \001Seconds on the Overpower icon\002 (on), under \001Time left on the Overpower icon\002. A crisp white number in the middle of the Overpower, Mongoose Bite or Riposte icon counts its seconds left down over the swipe, 5 to 1, and starts again at 5 when a new dodge or parry renews it. Setup's \001Marks and icons\002 drawing shows a 3, and test mode's sample counts down.",
			"Riposte and Mongoose Bite now show grey from your parry or dodge while energy or mana is short, and their swipe counts from it. Before, a parry right after a Sinister Strike showed nothing until energy ticked in, up to 2 seconds later, and the swipe then claimed a full 5 seconds that the game had already cut short. The icon now lights up and glows as the energy or mana arrives.",
			"New \001Full points breathe\002 (on), under \001Full points colour\002 on the \001Marks and icons\002 tab. While you sit at your most combo points, the gold row breathes softly, from full to about half and back about once a second, and stops the moment a finisher spends them, so full points are not wasted in a busy fight. Setup's \001Full points\002 sample and test mode's sample breathe the same way.",
			"\001Health text format\002 at \001Missing\002 now leaves an unhurt enemy's nameplate blank, as the unit frames do. Before, every full-health enemy showed a 0. A hurt enemy still shows how much it is missing (\"1.2K\"), and setup's full sample matches.",
			"\001Show health text\002 at \001Only on the target\002 or \001On the target and focus\002 now eases the health number out on the old target and in on the new one over 0.15 seconds, with the aura rows, instead of blinking on Tab. A nameplate that appears, and a change of the options, still set it at once.",
			"Dragging a game setting of the nameplate pages in a fight now says so once a fight, while the options window shows: \"Flat UI: nameplate game settings change when the fight ends.\". This covers the rows under \001Which show\002, \001Size and stacking\002 and \001Scale and fade\002, \001Aura size\002 and the class colour rows. Before, the slider could jump back with no word, and the change still lands as the fight ends.",
			"And 81 more changes in the full changelog on CurseForge.",
		},
	},
	{
		version = "0.5.43",
		lines = {
			"Threat list: a new List row. At Enemies while you tank (the default), a tank sees a row for each enemy with a nameplate that you or your group fight, instead of the group. Each row has the enemy's raid marker, its name and a bar of your hold on it, and the ones you are losing come first.",
			"Threat list: in Enemies, an enemy someone else holds is Lost. It is red, pulses fast and names who holds it in their class colour with their share of your threat (\"Mage 112%\"). One where another member passes Slipping at (80% by default) turns amber and pulses slowly, naming who is closing in. Enemies you hold stay green, calm and a little dimmer.",
			"Threat list: Enemies outlines your target's row, and the title reads \"Threat: 3 enemies, 1 lost\". A last line counts the enemies past Number of bars, and an enemy that turns to Lost flashes and plays Sound when you lose an enemy (by default Warning sound, while Warn when you lose aggro is on, as in the group list). An enemy another tank holds stays calm.",
			"Threat list: Enemies reads five times a second in a fight and nothing out of one. Its rows take no clicks; pointing at one says who has the enemy and to click its nameplate. Enemies you fight shows it in every role, and Your group on your target keeps the list as it was.",
			"Threat list: Animate bars (on by default). Bars slide to each new value, rows glide to their new place and flash as they overtake another, the top bar has a faint sheen, and your group's names take a light shade of their class colour.",
			"Threat list: test mode and /flatui threat show four sample enemies in Enemies, one lost, one slipping and two held, and in test mode their states turn every 6 seconds.",
			"Nameplates: Mark enemies you are losing (on by default). While you tank, an enemy changing hands or on someone else pulses with a soft glow in its threat colour, faster once it is lost, under a small arrow pointing down at it. The sample nameplates of test mode and Setup show it too.",
			"New \001Absorb shields on the health bar\002 (Bright layer over the health), in a new \001Absorb shields\002 section of the \001Personal resource display\002 page. Your absorb shields now lie over the display's health bar as a silvery layer that starts where your health ends, with a thin bright edge where the shield ends. A shield bigger than your missing health runs on back over your health, so its full size still shows on the bar; at full health it runs in from the right end. \001Plain fill after the health\002 stops it at the end of the bar instead, and \001Off\002 takes it away.",
			"The shield layer has a slow \001Sheen\002 (on) that sweeps across it every few seconds. It flashes as a new shield lands or grows and fades quickly as it breaks, though not while the game withholds the amount, and \001Shield outline\002 (on) frames the whole health bar in \001Shield colour\002 while a shield is up. \001Shield colour\002 (a light silver blue) and \001Shield opacity\002 (60%) set its look, and \001Shield amount\002 (off) adds the amount after the health text, as +1,234, in the shield colour.",
			"The shield on the health bar works in a fight and uses the game's absorb total. On a client without one it shows the shields the \001Absorb shields\002 page tracks. Test mode lays a sample shield of about 35% of your health over the display, with its sheen, and setup's \001Personal resource display\002 step lists the new rows.",
			"New \001What's new\002 window: from now on, the first login after Flat UI updates shows what changed in each version since the one you last played on this account, newest first, with setting names in the accent colour. This first time it shows 0.5.43, as older versions kept no note of the one you played. It has \001Open Flat UI's options\002 and \001Close\002, Escape closes it, and it can be dragged anywhere on screen. It waits for the end of a fight, and if a fight starts it steps aside (\"Flat UI: What's new comes back after the fight.\") and comes back after it. A new install shows nothing, as the welcome screen and setup walk you through Flat UI.",
			"New \001What's new after an update\002, under \001Updates\002 on the \001Options and setup\002 tab of the \001Flat UI\002 page: \001Show the window\002 (the default), \001One line in chat\002 (\"Flat UI updated to 0.5.43. /flatui new shows what changed.\") or \001Nothing\002. A character the welcome screen greets gets the chat line instead of the window.",
			"New /flatui new (or /flatui changes) opens \001What's new\002 at any time with this version's changes, and /flatui new 0.5.41 shows that version, one of the newest six. \001What's new in 0.5.43\002, at the bottom of \001Home\002 in the options window, opens it too.",
		},
	},
	{
		version = "0.5.42",
		lines = {
			"/flatui scale 80 now sets the scale it names. At the defaults it did nothing, because \001Scale below 65%\002 (on by default) sets the scale before \001Custom scale\002. From 65 to 115 it now turns \001Pixel-perfect scale\002 and \001Scale below 65%\002 off and uses \001Custom scale\002; from 35 to 64 it uses \001Small scale\002 with Scale below 65% on. Chat then gives the real result in the words of /flatui status, as \"Flat UI: Custom UI scale: 80%. /reload puts the game's scale back.\", and the reload notice shows when Scale below 65% goes off. /flatui scale off answers the same way.",
			"/flatui followed by words that are no command, as /flatui border colour, now opens the options window with those words in \001Search\002: the results show lit, Enter opens the first, and a miss shows the closest pages. Before, it printed the whole command list. /flatui help and /flatui ? still list the commands.",
			"Setup's \001Your screen is set\002 card can now take a \001Finish\002 back until you reload. After a save into the profile in use it shows \001Undo setup\002, which puts that profile back as it was, as \001Bring back\002 does (\"Flat UI: Raid is back as it was before setup.\", with \"Game settings setup changed stay as they are.\" when it wrote some). A Finish that made a new profile now shows \001Back to Default\002, as a ready-made look does. The Finish line in chat ends \"Undo setup on the card, or Profiles > Bring back, puts it back until you reload.\"",
			"Home's \001Needs a reload\002 \001Undo\002 now keeps the change in the record. \001Recent changes\002 shows the Undo as a line of its own, and Ctrl+Z (or that line's \001Undo\002) makes the change again, as the reload notice's Undo does. Before, the change vanished from Recent changes and Ctrl+Z stepped back to an older one. The strip now ends \"Ctrl+Z makes it again.\"",
			"The small reset arrow on a changed row now says what it did in the strip: \"Reset Border colour: back to #000000. Ctrl+Z puts it back.\" (\"back to its default\" where the value has no words of its own). The arrow brightens under the mouse, as the \001Search\002 box's cross does.",
			"The questions and tips of \001Delete profile\002, \001Reset profile\002, \001Copy from\002 and an import with \001Replace\002 no longer say \"for good\". Each now ends \"Bring back puts it back until you reload.\" (or \"them\"), as in 'Delete \"Alt\"? No other character uses it. Bring back puts it back until you reload.' They stay red, and Enter still does not answer them.",
			"Dragging \001Custom scale\002, or changing a row of the \001Fonts\002 page such as \001Text size\002 or \001Interface font\002, in a fight now says so, once a fight, while the options window shows: \"Flat UI: the UI scale changes when the fight ends.\" or \"Flat UI: fonts change when the fight ends.\". The change still lands as the fight ends.",
			"The sidebar now shows where your changes are: a small dot in the accent after the name of each module whose pages (its tabs and \001Per frame\002) hold a setting off its default. The dot goes once the last of them is back at its default. The \001Icon rail\002 layout shows no names and no dots.",
			"The \001Flat UI\002 page now has a preview band: setup's bordered panel with a health and a mana bar, in your border, background and \001Bar texture\002. Its \001Colours\002 tab shows setup's colour bars: health at 100%, 70%, 40% and 15%, the cast, channel, uninterruptible, interrupted and craft colours, reaction and power. Both follow each change at once.",
			"\001Switch automatically\002: \001Switch now\002 in a fight now waits for the fight's end, as a profile picked by hand does. The strip says \"Switches to Raid when the fight ends.\", the title bar shows \"Raid after the fight\", and the profile switches as the fight ends. Before, it only said \"Profiles change after combat.\" and nothing waited.",
			"New \001Fade as one runs out\002 (on), under \001Squares for your heals over time\002. Each square now fades from full to about a third over the last 3 seconds of its heal over time, so a healer sees which Renew or Rejuvenation to refresh next. It goes back to full when the spell is recast. In a fight it fades by the end time read before the pull, and a square that never ends stays full. Setup's Bex shows the last square half faded.",
			"Heal over time squares on party frames draw at the default \001Square size\002 6 again at one pixel a unit. Since 0.5.41 they drew as 5, which left almost half less colour inside the rim. A square now stops one pixel short of the bar's edge, so sizes 7 to 14 picked for raid cells still stay inside the 10-unit party bar. Setup's Bex matches.",
			"New \001Five-second rule\002 (off), on the \001Player frame\002 tab. After each spell that costs mana, a thin bright spark crosses your mana bar from left to right over 5 seconds and starts again at the next cast. When it reaches the end, Spirit is refilling your mana again. It works in a fight, on the game's bar or Flat UI's, and a druid in Bear or Cat Form sees it on the druid mana bar. Test mode loops it, and setup's \001Player and target frames\002 step draws it 40% of the way across the mana bar.",
			"\001Dim members out of range\002 now darkens only the member's own picture, name row and bars (their body in \001One frame\002). Before, it also darkened the top of their debuff icons and pet frame, which left a half-dark strip. A party pet's frame now dims by its own range. With \001Border for your target\002 at Own colour, the outline wraps the same part, just clear of the picture's border, and no longer runs through the pet frame and the debuff row.",
			"With \001Picture\002 None in \001Separate parts\002, as the \001Healer\002 look sets it, the party marks now use the empty picture square. The raid marker sits at its top, the resurrection icon at full size in its middle and AFK at its bottom, clear of the name row and the health text. In \001One frame\002, where None removes the square, they stay on the health bar.",
			"\001AFK on party frames\002 now fades in and out. After the first minute it counts the time away, \"AFK 3m\" and later \"AFK 1h\", from when Flat UI first saw the member go, so a group waiting at the boss knows whether to wait or pull.",
			"New \001AFK on raid frames\002 (on). Raid cells and raid-style party frames show the same soft grey AFK, fading and counting the same way, where the game's status text sits and at its size. It works in a fight too and hides while the member is dead or offline. Setup's \001Boss and raid frames\002 step shows it on Eli.",
			"Setup's \001Party frames\002 step now draws \001AFK on party frames\002. Dru, already drawn out of range, shows a grey AFK at the bottom of his picture, or at the left of his bar with \001Picture\002 None in \001One frame\002, and pointing at the row brackets it. The row moved there from \001Auras, flags and icons\002.",
			"Setup: \001Role icons for\002 moved to the \001Party frames\002 step. Cole, Dru and Fay lose their icons with Tanks and healers, and Mara loses hers with Healers only. The bracket is on Bex's healer icon, which every choice keeps. Before, picking a choice in \001Auras, flags and icons\002 changed nothing in its drawing.",
			"Some changes made in a fight now say once in chat, while the options window is open, that they apply when the fight ends, as the heal over time squares already did. This covers \001Dim members out of range\002 (\"Flat UI: party members dim by range when the fight ends.\"), \001Low health border\002, a \001Health text\002 or \001Power text\002 format, and the party and raid marks: the AFK words, \001Resurrection icon on party frames\002, the raid markers, the leader icons and \001Border for your target\002 (\"Flat UI: party and raid frame marks change when the fight ends.\"). Each change still applies at the end of the fight. A change raised with the window closed, such as zoning or a new member, stays silent.",
			"New \001Flash as you change target\002 (on), under \001Target and focus\002 on the \001Marks and icons\002 tab of \001Nameplates\002: as you change target, the new target's health bar border flashes once in \001Target colour\002. It is a thicker outline that fades over about a third of a second and leaves the normal target border, also in a fight, so with Tab in a pack you see at once which nameplate it took. Setting a focus flashes the focus's nameplate in \001Focus colour\002. Setup's Target and Focus samples flash as the drawing opens and as the row turns on.",
			"Rogues: the Overpower row is now \001Overpower, Mongoose Bite or Riposte on enemy nameplates\002. A rogue who knows \001Riposte\002 sees its icon pop over their target's nameplate as it becomes usable after they parry. It glows for its 5 seconds (a parry of yours renews them), is grey while only energy is short, follows a change of target and goes as you cast it, with the same swipe, pop and fade as Mongoose Bite. Setup's \001Marks and icons\002 drawing shows it for a rogue who knows Riposte.",
			"New \001Nameplates on enemy pets and totems\002 (off), \001Nameplates on minor enemies\002 (off) and \001Friendly players as names only\002 (off), under \001Which show\002 on the \001Size and clicks\002 tab of \001Nameplates\002. These are the game's own settings, so the clutter of totems and hunter pets, or friendly players' health bars, can go without leaving Flat UI. Each shows the game's value and writes nothing until you move it, as the other game rows of that tab do.",
			"Setup's \001Nameplate auras\002 and \001Nameplate text\002 steps, and the preview bands of the \001Auras\002 and \001Text\002 tabs, now draw \001Show auras\002 and \001Show health text\002. At \001Only on the target\002 the auras and the health number stay on a \001Your target\002 sample alone, and the plain enemy beside it goes bare. \001On the target and focus\002 adds a \001Focus\002 sample that keeps them. Pointing at either row brackets the sample that keeps them.",
			"Setup's \001Marks and icons\002 drawing for a rogue or druid has a second combo sample, \001Full points\002: all five squares, in \001Full points colour\002 while \001Full points stand out\002 is on. Dragging the colour repaints it at once, the row pops together as \001Full points stand out\002 turns on, and pointing at either row brackets it.",
			"Setup's \001Nameplate colours and threat\002 step and the \001Threat\002 tab's preview band now draw a fifth sample, \001Held by another tank\002, while you count as a tank with \001Enemies held by another tank\002 on. It is in \001Another tank's colour\002, fades like an enemy held for you, has no ring at \001When the enemy needs you\002 and shows 60%. Pointing at \001Enemies held by another tank\002 or \001Another tank's colour\002 brackets it.",
			"Fix: with \001Your role\002 at \001From your role, stance or form\002, setup's threat samples now work your role out afresh (group role, Main Tank, a tank aura, Defensive Stance, Bear Form, Righteous Fury). They show the colours your nameplates will take after \001Finish\002, not those of the role you had picked before.",
			"Fix: turning \001Restyle friendly nameplates\002 off or on in a fight now says in chat, once a fight while the options window shows, \"Flat UI: nameplates take their new size when the fight ends.\", as the other size rows do. Before, the change seemed to do nothing until the fight ended.",
			"With \001Show auras\002 at \001Only on the target\002 or \001On the target and focus\002, each change of target or focus now eases the old row of auras out and the new one in over 0.15 s, as the threat rings and combo points fade, so Tab no longer makes the debuff rows blink. A nameplate that shows and a change of the options still set them at once.",
			"Tips for the \001Nameplates\002 rows that had none: \001Restyle friendly nameplates\002, \001Own border and background\002, \001Incoming heals\002, \001Absorb shields\002, \001Heal absorbs\002, \001Colour health bars by health left\002, \001Only in dungeons and raids\002, \001Show threat value\002, the three \001Enemy's target\002 rows, \001Level box shape\002, \001Level box gap\002, \001Class icon size\002, \001Raid marker\002, \001Arrow for nameplates behind you\002, \001Shield on uninterruptible casts\002, \001Aura opacity\002, \001Another tank's colour\002, \001Extra clickable width\002 and \001Extra clickable height\002. Each says in one sentence what it changes.",
			"Cast bars: the spell icon left of your cast bar and your pet's, with \001Spell icon on player and pet bars\002, now stays on the bar through its finish fade and its 1.3 s Interrupted hold, with or without \001Flat fill\002, so you still see which spell was kicked. Before, it went the moment the cast ended, and no icon showed during the Interrupted hold. The latency zone still goes as the cast ends.",
			"\001Target of enemy casts\002: the \"> Bex\" past the target or focus cast bar now stays while the bar fades or holds Interrupted, so you can still read who the kicked Polymorph was aimed at. It goes as the bar hides or the next cast starts. Setup's picture and test mode keep the icon, the name and the pushback in the Interrupted state too, as the live bars do.",
			"New \001Seconds until your interrupt is ready\002 (on), on the \001Text and icon\002 tab of \001Cast bars\002: while an enemy you target or focus casts and your Kick, Counterspell or Pummel cools down, a small \"4\" counts down inside the left end of its cast bar in \001Interrupt on cooldown colour\002. You see at a glance whether Kick is back before the 3.5 s Fireball lands, and the number goes as the bar turns \001Interrupt ready colour\002. It works with or without \001Flat fill\002, in a fight too, while \001Interrupt readiness on enemy casts\002 is on, and setup's \001Focus\002 row draws a sample.",
			"New \001Channel tick colour\002 (white at first) under \001Channel ticks on the player bar\002: the tick marks of Mind Flay, Arcane Missiles or Drain Life take it at 60% opacity on the live bar, in setup's picture and in test mode. A priest with \001Class colour on the player bar\002 can now pick a dark colour that shows on the white bar.",
			"Combat text: with \001Spell names\002 on \001After every spell\002, a named number in the Outgoing area now takes a lane as wide as its name, and only the two lanes away from your character. An Arcane Explosion, Multi-Shot, Consecration or Hellfire tick on four enemies now reads as two clean columns instead of names printed over each other, and the rest wait their turn.",
			"New \001Sound when your focus casts\002 (None at first, with the same eight choices), under \001Sound\002 on the \001Cast bars\002 page: it plays as the enemy you focus starts a cast or channel while your interrupt is ready and in range, at most once a second, so you hear \"kick now\" without watching the focus bar. It needs \001Interrupt readiness on enemy casts\002, and picking a sound plays it once.",
			"Combat text: test mode's Outgoing sample and /flatui combat test now write \"824 Fireball\" (the client's own name of the spell) while \001Whose hits\002 is \001Yours\002 and \001Spell names\002 is \001After every spell\002, as setup's picture does. Pointing at \001Spell names\002 in setup now marks the 510 Fireball sample.",
			"Combat alerts: \001Low health\002 and \001Low mana\002 now breathe in step with \001No target\002, one shared cycle, instead of dimming while the other brightens. As the fight ends they fade out over a quarter second with No target instead of vanishing in one frame, so \001-Combat\002 pops in a calm screen.",
			"Test mode: the first sample Interrupted of the player cast bar in a session now plays your \001Sound when you are interrupted\002 once, so you can judge it with the bar in view. None plays nothing.",
			"Tips on \001Flat fill\002, \001Class colour on the player bar\002, \001Own colours on target and focus bars\002, \001Border takes the cast colour\002, \001Target and focus bar icon\002 and the four custom cast bar sizes on the \001Cast bars\002 pages, and on \001Flat fill\002, \001Fill from the right\002 and \001Out of range opacity\002 of \001Swing timers\002. Each now says in one line what it does and what depends on it.",
			"And 41 more changes in the full changelog on CurseForge.",
